    "text": "To determine the value of \\( y \\) that is not attainable for the function \\( y = \\frac{1-x}{2x+3} \\), we need to identify the value of \\( y \\) that makes the denominator zero, as division by zero is undefined.\n\nThe denominator of the function is \\( 2x + 3 \\). We set the denominator equal to zero and solve for \\( x \\):\n\n\\[\n2x + 3 = 0\n\\]\n\nSubtract 3 from both sides:\n\n\\[\n2x = -3\n\\]\n\nDivide both sides by 2:\n\n\\[\nx = -\\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis value of \\( x \\) makes the denominator zero, and thus the function is undefined at \\( x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\). Therefore, the value of \\( y \\) that is not attainable is the value of \\( y \\) when \\( x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\).\n\nTo find this value, we substitute \\( x = -\\frac{3}{2} \\) into the function:\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{1 - \\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{2\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) + 3}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the numerator and the denominator:\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{1 + \\frac{3}{2}}{-3 + 3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{\\frac{2}{2} + \\frac{3}{2}}{0}\n\\]\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{\\frac{5}{2}}{0}\n\\]\n\nSince division by zero is undefined, the value of \\( y \\) that is not attainable is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{undefined}}\n\\]",
